---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The Certificate"
  eyebrow="Senj registration 152/1954"
  dek="A sworn English translation of a Yugoslav birth extract — the one state document in this archive, and the only thing here that confirms Hedviga's birth from outside the family tree.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">It was attached to Hedviga's MyHeritage profile the whole time. It is
    <strong>not in the GEDCOM</strong> — media does not export — so the first build of this archive
    said plainly that no civil registration document underpinned anything. That was true of the data
    and false of the evidence, and this page is the correction.</p>
    <figure class="doc">
      <img src={u("/photos/hedviga/hedviga-04-501105-ivan-birth-certificate-translated.jpg")}
           alt="Sworn English translation of the Yugoslav birth extract for Ivan Defranceski, Senj, 1951"
           loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Extract from the register of births, Senj — issued 27 April 1954, translated for
      the Supreme Court of South Africa. 480 px rendering; the original is on MyHeritage.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What it says</h2>
    <blockquote class="note">TRANSLATION FROM THE CROATIAN ORIGINAL · FEDERAL NATIONAL REPUBLIC OF
    YUGOSLAVIA · NATIONAL REPUBLIC OF CROATIA · Region: Senj · National Register of Senj
    Municipality · Reg. number 152/1954</blockquote>
    <table class="kv" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <tr><th>Child</th><td><strong>DEFRANCESKI, IVAN</strong>, male — born
        <strong>17 December 1951 at 11:30</strong>, <strong>Senj Hospital</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Entered</th><td>1951 register, page 252, ordinal number 110, on
        <strong>22 December 1951</strong>, on the written notice of the Regional Hospital, Senj,
        no. 595/51</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Father</th><td><strong>Ivan Defranceski</strong> — born <strong>25.06.1925,
        Crikvenica</strong>; citizenship Croatian/Yugoslav; nationality <strong>Italian</strong>;
        profession <strong>electro-mechanic</strong>; resident Crikvenica</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Mother</th><td><strong>Hedviga, <em>née</em> Blazevic</strong> — born
        <strong>03.08.1926, Senj</strong>; citizenship FNRJ; nationality Croatian; profession
        <strong>housewife</strong>; resident Crikvenica</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Issued</th><td>Senj, <strong>27 April 1954</strong>. Registrar
        <strong>Slavković Krsto</strong>, under the seal of the Municipality of Senj</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Certified</th><td><strong>Marijana Čizek</strong>, Sworn Translator for the
        <strong>Supreme Court of South Africa</strong></td></tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Four things it settles or opens</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Hedviga's birth, confirmed from outside the tree</h3>
        <p><strong>3 August 1926, Senj.</strong> Every other statement of that date in this archive
        traces back to the same MyHeritage record. This one is a state register entry, written down
        in 1951 by a registrar who was recording her son's birth and had no interest in her
        genealogy. <a href={u("/method/")}>Why that distinction is the whole method →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The father is recorded as <em>Italian</em></h3>
        <p>Yugoslav civil registration separated <em>citizenship</em> from <em>nationality</em>, and
        Ivan Defranceski is entered as a Yugoslav citizen of <strong>Italian</strong> nationality —
        while Hedviga, in the next column, is <strong>Croatian</strong>. The tree records no such
        thing. It is consistent with the <em>de Franceschi</em> spelling the family carries.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>They were living at Crikvenica</h3>
        <p>Both parents are resident at <strong>Crikvenica</strong> in December 1951, though the
        birth was at Senj hospital — which is why Ladislava (1948) is recorded born at Crikvenica
        and the two boys at Senj. The family was at Crikvenica and went up the coast to the
        hospital. <a href={u("/hedviga/")}>Hedviga →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>It is emigration paperwork</h3>
        <p>A Yugoslav birth extract gets translated by a <strong>sworn translator for the Supreme
        Court of South Africa</strong> for one reason: an official application. The extract was
        drawn in <strong>1954</strong>. That is roughly twenty years before Hedviga's recorded
        arrival in South Africa in April 1975 — so either the translation is much later than the
        extract, or the family's move out of Yugoslavia began far earlier than this archive
        assumed. <strong>The translation is undated in what can be read here.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>What would settle the last one.</strong> The
    original document, at full resolution, to read the translator's date stamp; and the South
    African immigration file the translation was made for.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
